# archive.list -- file archivers, compressors and serial file transfer.  All
# period third-party ports that shipped for COHERENT on the MWC BBS; sources in
# os/archive and os/comms, built by hostbuild/build-archive.sh,
# hostbuild/build-gzip.sh and hostbuild/build-comms.sh.
#
# Everything here goes in /usr/bin.  Read base.list's note on the /bin versus
# /usr/bin split before moving any of it: /bin is what the single-user
# maintenance shell can reach, and none of these is a repair tool.  gzip is the
# largest single cost, because its three names are three copies of the same 97
# blocks rather than links -- check the `mkimage' block counts before adding
# more.

# --- archivers --------------------------------------------------------------
# zoo 2.1 (Rahul Dhesi, 1991).  Creates and extracts.  Its `a' and `x' are the
# common cases, `l' lists and `P' packs an archive down.  Packing matters here:
# when compression makes a member LARGER, zoo rewrites it stored over the same
# offset and cannot shorten the file afterwards, so an archive of incompressible
# data carries the failed attempt's tail as slack.  The archive still lists and
# extracts correctly; `zoo P' removes the slack.
#
#   2.1, NOT 2.01, and the reason is the licence.  Dhesi's statement for 2.00
#   and 2.01 permits distribution "for noncommercial purposes" only and forbids
#   shipping compiled code without the source; his 1991 statement for 2.1
#   permits copying "for any purpose, whether commercial or noncommercial".
#   This repository publishes a disk image, so that difference is load-bearing.
#   2.1 also adds LZH (method 2) alongside the 13-bit LZW, which is what takes
#   the binary from 64 KB to 90 KB -- about 54 blocks more of /usr.
# PACKAGE (os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT).  A list with a `package' line is an
# addressable unit of a release channel; one without is not, and that is how
# testing.list stays out of one.
package  archive
role     optional
# KINDS -- the packages this component publishes besides its runtime half.
# `bin' is the list below and is not written down; `src' is the complete
# corresponding source for every executable it installs, and `man' is the pages
# for those programs.  os/dist/PACKAGE-FORMAT argues both, and neither can be
# cut from a guess: -src refuses without the producer's source map, -man refuses
# when not one of these programs has a page.
kinds    src man
# ARTICLES -- kermit is C-Kermit installed under the protocol's name, and the
# Lexicon documents it as ckermit (the vendor's own page, comms/man); the
# article is claimed for the program so the -man package carries it.
articles kermit ckermit

f	/usr/bin/zoo		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/zoo
# lharc 1.00 / C-LHarc (Y. Tagawa, K. U. Rommel, 1989-90).  LZHUF, and it
# compresses appreciably better than zoo's LZW -- 46 KB against 53 KB on the
# same 166 KB test set.  The COHERENT configuration defines NODIRECTORY, so it
# archives the files it is named and does NOT descend into directories.
# Terms: os/archive/lharc/COPYING (Tagawa's "This is FREEWARE"), and NOTICE.md.
f	/usr/bin/lharc		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/lharc
# unzip 4.1 (Info-ZIP, 1991).  EXTRACT ONLY -- the matching creator is zip(1),
# which is not built.  It reads stored, shrunk, reduced and imploded members,
# which is every method PKZIP 1.x produced and therefore every method the .zip
# files in the archives on this system use.  It predates deflate, so it cannot
# read a zipfile written by a modern zip.
# Terms: os/archive/unzip/COPYING (Smith 1989, and Info-ZIP's own record of what
# he intended), and NOTICE.md -- 5.41+ is the licensed successor, not yet ported.
f	/usr/bin/unzip		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/unzip

# --- compressors ------------------------------------------------------------
# gzip 0.8.2 (archive/gzip), under three names.
#
# gzip opens its output O_CREAT|O_EXCL, so it depends on the kernel clearing
# its in-core exclusive-open mark at close (sys/coh/fs3.c iclose(), covered
# by tests/openexcl); a kernel that does not refuses every later open of the
# archive with EEXIST until the inode is evicted.
#
#   THE THIRD NAME MUST BE gzcat, NOT zcat: base.list already stages /bin/zcat
#   (a copy of compress(1), LZW only), and gzip.c:351 already recognises
#   "gzcat".  A duplicate destination is a hard error in the packer, so the
#   name has to differ even though the directories now do.
f	/usr/bin/gzip
f	/usr/bin/gunzip	src=hostbuild/build/bin/gzip
f	/usr/bin/gzcat	src=hostbuild/build/bin/gzip

# --- serial file transfer ---------------------------------------------------
# rz/sz (Chuck Forsberg, Omen Technology).  ZMODEM, with YMODEM and XMODEM
# fallbacks.  cu(1) dials a line; these move files over one.  Upstream links
# each under three names -- the base name speaks ZMODEM, the `b' name YMODEM
# and the `x' name XMODEM -- and the programs switch on argv[0], so the extra
# names are not decoration, they are how the other protocols are selected.
f	/usr/bin/sz		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/sz
f	/usr/bin/sb		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/sz
f	/usr/bin/sx		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/sz
f	/usr/bin/rz		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/rz
f	/usr/bin/rb		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/rz
f	/usr/bin/rx		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/rz
# minirb: a small XMODEM receiver, the bootstrap for a machine that has no
# transfer program yet -- type it in over a dumb line, then use it to pull rz
# and sz across.
f	/usr/bin/minirb		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/minirb

# C-Kermit 4E(072).  KERMIT protocol over a serial line, and the interactive
# client (`kermit c') for a line cu(1) is not wanted on.  128334 bytes, 251
# blocks: it fits /usr on both the 21 MB and the 42 MB media.
f	/usr/bin/kermit		755 0 1	src=hostbuild/build/bin/kermit
